<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.11.4: Writing Quadratics When Given Any Format</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02279</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.11.4: Writing Quadratics When Given Any Format</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8212db4e-dd22-4483-a230-dd200f838fcc</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para>In this lesson, you have learned how to write a quadratic function from different starting contexts and when provided different information.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Identify a quadratic function in standard form, \(b(x)\), with the zeros –6 and 8.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form, \(b(x)\).</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(b(x) = x^2 − 2x − 48\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Identify a quadratic function in standard form, \(m(x)\), with the given zero.</para>
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH AN \(x\)-intercepts OF 6.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_13_09_01.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
         </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form, \(m(x)\).</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(m(x) = x^2 − 12x + 36\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write the specific quadratic function, \(d(x)\), in standard form, that has the zeros 2 and 5 and passes through the point \((1, 16)\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter the quadratic function, \(d(x)\), in standard form.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Intercept form: \(d(x) = a(x − 2)(x − 5)\)</para>
          <para>Find the value of \(a\). Use point \((1, 16)\).</para>
          <para>\(d(x) = a(x − 2)(x − 5)\)
      \(16 = a((1) − 2)((1) − 5)\)
      \(16 = a(-1)(-4)\)
      \(16 = 4a\)
      \(4 = a\)</para>
          <para>Substitute 4 for \(a\).</para>
          <para>\(d(x) = a(x − 2)(x − 5)\)
      \(d(x) = 4(x − 2)(x − 5)\)
      \(d(x) = 4(x^2 − 2x − 5x + 10)\)
      \(d(x) = 4(x^2 − 7x + 10)\)
      \(d(x) = 4x^2 − 28x + 40\)</para>
          <para>So, the specific quadratic function written in standard form is \(d(x) = 4x^2 − 28x + 40\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
